# Monday, August 11

At last, I find myself in a café opposite the Pamplona bus station. It’s 9:47 in the morning and I’m having a Spanish omelette sandwich and a coffee with milk. I’m going to try to put into words what happened yesterday and the day before yesterday.

Since the bus to St. Jean Pied de Port doesn’t leave until 12:00, I think I’ll have enough time. Although I also need to buy a hat; I’m even considering cutting my hair really short.

On Friday night, August 8, there was the Bhuo concert and it was really great.

The next day, Saturday the 9th, I went to the Bio Music One session with Jean-Louis, and it also had quite a liberating effect. I noticed images of the people I had been sharing experiences with coming to my mind. Like the guy who plays guitar, the Argentinian girl who does acrobatics, who taught me how to hold her in the air using only my legs, supporting her abdomen and hips. And many more people with different abilities. With another song, I imagined a jellyfish-shaped balloon shooting out from me toward the sea, gliding across the surface and leaving a trail with droplets of water suspended in the air before rising and disappearing into the horizon. At the end of the session, when the music stopped, I remained lying there while things still felt as though they were moving inside me.

I asked him what what I saw meant, and he told me it was energy my subconscious needed to release there. However, neither the astrologer nor certain other people appeared in those visions.

The morning workshop, “Energy Harmonization,” was led by Luis Paniagua, with his lyre made from a turtle shell and antelope horns, an instrument as unique as he is. He explained the origin of the lyre, rooted in Greek mythology.

The workshop felt magical. He taught us to sing harmonics using vowels following a very spiritual melody. His music reminded me of “Vuela Vuela” by Moni. The chakras, in order to harmonize, need the body to vibrate at a certain frequency focused on a specific part of the body. The sounds go from bottom to top: “M,” “O,” “U,” “A,” “E,” “I,” and finally “S” for silence. The chakras are root, sacral, solar plexus, heart, throat, third eye, and crown.

The practice consisted of filling our lungs completely and singing the vowel until all the air was gone. But because there were so many people, the vibration continued uninterrupted the whole time: something very special. A tear escaped me when, after singing the “U,” there was a sacred silence during which we heard birds singing. Luis identified them as eagles. In that moment I turned to my partner on the right, “Begona.” Right now I realize the partner on my left was also called Begona—the DJ. I’ll talk about her later. I told the first Begona that we were speaking the language of nature, and that frequency was resonating with the language of the eagles, and they were responding to us in recognition, saying: “Yes, we hear you. Thank you.”

In the afternoon I attended the tantra workshop with a very sweet and special facilitator. I arrived a bit late because I wasn’t sure whether to go or not, but it didn’t matter because they were still doing the introduction. She guided us chakra by chakra, activating the energy through subtle movements focused on each point: hips, legs, chest, arms. It was very fun because for the lower chakras I felt more comfortable doing twisting exercises on the floor, and as the focus moved upward I stood up and used hip movements. She had us move around and find someone to dance with. I ended up with a girl and they played a salsa song, hahaha, and we danced a little.

Later, to open the heart chakra, I did the bridge pose and managed to hold it for a few seconds (honestly, I’ve pushed my body to its limits this week), which has left me sore but satisfied at the same time.

We finished with the crown chakra, lying on the floor and relaxing all that energy, letting it flow through the entire body. Then we paired up and, when I opened my eyes, I saw two masculine silhouettes (because I wasn’t wearing glasses), and on the left there was only one woman, to whom I extended my hand and she extended hers to me. We came together and melted into an embrace full of peace, calm, harmony, and sensory pleasure. After a while, the facilitator suggested we either stay with that person or switch partners if we weren’t comfortable. We decided to stay together.

We then began the “tantric massage” practice (with clearly defined boundaries): first hands and arms, then back, feet, legs, sides of the hips, back again, neck, head, and face. First face down, then face up. Afterwards we switched roles. I started first and, in the end, when it was her turn, she touched my body. I felt a deeply pleasurable sensation. We ended the practice sitting opposite each other, sharing the experience, followed by a long embrace in which I felt incredibly comfortable. After the workshop we sat together on the grass chatting. She told me she planned to suggest the massage practice to some friends and that maybe, if I ever go to Madrid, perhaps I should call her. I won’t mention her name, but for me she will be “The Magician of Tactile Senses.”

During dinner I don’t really remember who I sat with, since I kept sitting with different people and some conversations impacted me more than others.

Saturday night was very special, a kind of open mic night. Between the workshop and dinner there had also been a poetry session in which I would have liked to participate, but I think I stayed too long enjoying the conversation with the Magician of Tactile Senses and ran out of time. During that conversation she told me she loved Luis Paniagua and saw him as a wise druid of the ancient Celts—and truly, he is. A man with very strong energy, great sensitivity, and wisdom. I told her a little about my personal spiritual practice and spoke to her about Lama Rinchen and the Paramita group, which she already knew about, and I mentioned the new center in Madrid.

At the open mic, some artists among the participants performed. There was a lot of talent. The salsa guy asked me for a favor and of course I did it. He wanted me to record his performance with the band called “The Guys from the Queue at the Lower Dining Hall.” I thought he wanted me to record the whole thing, so I stood in the center filming. Thoughts crossed my mind: there I was, arms raised filming, unable to fully enjoy the experience, while he wasn’t even performing. I thought, “If he’s not on stage, shouldn’t he be the one recording?” Then after two performances where he still hadn’t appeared, I overheard a sarcastic comment that made me laugh: “Careful with the guy filming—he’s in a terrible mood!” (XD)

When I finally went to tell him I was tired of recording, he went on stage. I recorded the performance, and after three songs I returned the camera and he thanked me. Then I dedicated the merit for the benefit of all beings.

Just when it seemed to be ending and I was in the tea area, suddenly a DJ started playing techno-house-spiritual music that sounded as if it had descended from heaven. I went over there and once again let myself be carried away by the music. It was incredible, magical—the way she mixed songs and transitions. A true master of the mixing desk. It was Begona, the girl with the harmonic drum, the unusual face and beautiful smile, from Valencia. It felt like a gift for the soul—or for the mental continuum.

Sunday the 10th—the farewell ceremony, lunch, the trip to Pamplona, and my first encounters with pilgrims—I’ll tell that story the next time I sit down to write. Very soon.

It’s now 18:08 and I’m already settled into the municipal hostel or refuge in Saint-Jean-Pied-de-Port.

Continuing with the 10th: around 4:30 or 5 in the morning, I heard dogs barking fiercely and the cries of eagles, as if some kind of animal fight were taking place. Later, asking around, nobody else had heard anything, but someone told me one participant had gone walking and found a dead sheep along the path. With that full moon, I’m not surprised. So there’s the explanation.

I went to the final yoga session and it felt very restorative. At breakfast I sat next to a Galician man with a rather peculiar sense of humor. Then I brushed my teeth and dressed in white for the closing ceremony. Though before that, on my own, I had already started saying goodbye to people with whom I felt I had shared something meaningful.

Among them was Izarbe, a rather short brunette with reddish hair and an angelic smile. The first time I saw her I really liked her, and I asked whether she was part of the staff because I had confused her with the singer from the cacao ceremony.

We said goodbye with a very heartfelt hug. I also ran into Ángel, then a tall man with sad-looking eyes who, in one of the workshops, symbolized my father and Fabregat. Soraya Ima, Rocío, the man with the hammer, Víctor with the chakra tattoo on his back, and several others.

During the ceremony I clearly remember two exchanges. A girl whose name I can’t remember, but who I think was my tent neighbor, thanked me simply for being myself. It touched me so deeply that I told her it isn’t easy at all, and I hugged her again with tears in my eyes.

The other was Fernando, a young man who had been sexually abused by his father and grandfather. After looking into his eyes for a few minutes, I told him: “I’ve seen the universe in your eyes.” They were green with blue sparkles, with a gaze that was both challenging and calm. I explained that the lines looked like galaxies becoming smaller and smaller toward the center—the pupil—and that he didn’t need to search for anything outside himself because he was already complete. Everything he needs is already within him.

We hugged and I joked, “Nothing gay about it, eh!” meaning it as a message from heart to heart. He laughed and said he had understood it that way already.

I said that because just before the closing ceremony began, everyone was packing up, and a few tents away there was a couple of guys laughing. I went over to see what was happening and saw a yogurt exploded everywhere. One of them said: “Wow, you waited seven days for that and now it happens in a second.” XD

Anyway, in the sharing circles I met Carol and thanked her for listening to my confessions.

After the circle I found Ainhoa, my tantra partner, who was talking with Luis Paniagua. I hugged her and thanked her for her ointment, and I also took the opportunity to say a few words to Luis. I told him the eagle-and-mantras anecdote and apologized for abandoning his teachings on Greek mythology. He understood and said I had done the right thing. Then Ainhoa took an amazing photo of the moment.

I also ran into the Japanese woman, Kiko. I apologized to her and she said there was no need. I asked her about Ikigai and she told me that in Japan it’s something very common—like saying “lentils.”

I met Jean-Louis again, who introduced me to the art of perceiving the energies of trees and telluric lines. We began with the guardian tree, with a thick trunk, one moss-covered side (north) and the other bark, representing the duality of all things. Looking up at it from behind, it resembled a woman with two breasts and her left arm raised toward the sky. I asked permission to enter the forest and gave thanks.

Halfway through the walk he explained telluric lines to me: temples and dolmens built on energetic points. He told me the churches spread across France form the shape of a rose.

I told him about pranic nourishment, and he explained that the shaman Henry Monfort ended up in prison because a woman died during one of his retreats, and apparently he made a lot of money too. Jean-Louis admitted he had wanted to try it himself.

He showed me the “gateway tree” and an area where one could perceive a change in energy. Further ahead, before reaching the tipi at the end, there was another tree from the same family as the guardian tree. We remained in contact and silence for several minutes, and I noticed a serpentine line moving inside the tree. Suddenly something seemed to flow from the tree toward me, like the shimmering movement you see above a road on a very hot day.

Afterwards I explained the experience to him, and then we met Carlota Sánchez—the girl who had collided with me during the workshop and who will always stay in my memory. She hugged me and kissed my neck lightly. I remained centered within myself without falling into the mundane, and I felt the tree’s energy flowing through me into her, as if I myself were the tree: still, unmoving, serene.

I understood then that nobody steals or gives energy to anyone or anything; rather, people, objects, and everything around us are all part of an energetic flow. What comes goes, and vice versa.

Before the giant paella meal, I met a woman wearing a pendant with a rune engraved on an orange stone. Looking into her eyes, I sensed tremendous strength, intensity, sincerity, and wisdom. She told me she had bought a magic wand and charged it together with a roommate friend who had independently bought another one. During the full moon night, they performed a ritual, making wishes or casting a spell upon the wand as a channeling object of energy. Franchesca, the shaman with the wand, knew what she was doing when she charged that stick with supernatural power.

The paella was delicious, and the trip to Pamplona with Alomai and María was wonderful. We stopped for coffee in a colorful little village, then continued to a tiny hamlet where Aitor and his wife live—the therapist of Alomai. A lovely couple who welcomed us with open arms. You know when you arrive, but not when you leave.

During the journey we listened to a podcast by someone called Alexandre Exot. His voice sounded familiar from Instagram posts. We listened to two episodes: “Sharing” and “The Secret of Women.” Both were very interesting, though I don’t think I’ll become a member of the channel. Paramita is enough for me.

When we arrived at their house in the blue C3, perfectly maintained like the one I used to own, it brought back many memories of my silver C3. We dropped off María and the luggage and went to fill up with petrol. I asked how much I owed and then we said goodbye.

I arrived at the hostel and found another pilgrim in front of me checking in. When he finished, it was my turn. Apparently everything is done with QR codes now. The man behind the desk was named Rashid, from Morocco. His eyes lit up the moment I asked his name and told him mine. I think he needed a bit of conversation. I mentioned that my supervisor was Palestinian, though we didn’t speak much because the other pilgrim returned saying someone’s backpack was on his bed. It turned out there had been a mistake.

In the end I was assigned the top bunk, but the other pilgrim told me, “Take the bottom one, there’s nobody there.” I listened to him and we started talking. My heart energy was still completely open from the festival. I looked at him and listened with all my senses. His name is Iván, from the Basque Country. He was doing the Camino as therapy. He has mental health problems, and his mother didn’t understand him and took away his medication. His wife left him and he can’t see or speak with his daughters.

I stood up and, when I saw emotion welling in his eyes, I hugged him. It was a very special moment. First “Apapacho” in real life.

He told me stories about having worked as a cook in Catalonia, about going to therapy because he himself asked for help, and I shared with him the story of my beloved little cousin. He also told me that until recently Iceland had a law allowing Basques to be killed. In any case, it was fascinating.

I gave him the hug from the sense of security I had gained during the festival. We all have fears and insecurities, the same nature, and the same desire to be happy. But until you look directly into someone’s eyes from a place of introspection, you know it intellectually but you don’t truly realize it.

I could verify that later. While I was talking with Iván, an older man arrived saying Iván was in his bunk and asking who had moved his belongings. He seemed suspicious and uncomfortable. So I stood up, asked his name, gave him mine, and said we were all pilgrims, there were many empty beds, and if necessary I could move. But Iván didn’t want that.

The man asked if we knew each other and I said, “No, we just met now, just like we’ve just met you.” He smiled and said, “These youngsters,” apologized, picked up his things, and moved to another bed.

This morning I realized I had lost my Casio wristwatch. I told the hostel worker that if he found it, to keep it for me.

I went to the bus station and right behind it found a park where, thanks to the sensitivity I had developed, I found some trees beneath which I sat to meditate. As I began the practice, I heard the sound of sprinklers, which made me stop and move to a safer area. I’m happy because I’ve resumed my practice. Let’s see if I can continue tomorrow, since I plan to leave around 4:30 in the morning to avoid the heat on the Camino.

I went to a café opposite the station for breakfast: a tortilla sandwich and coffee with milk. I spent a long time writing because I needed to catch up.

Afterwards I headed to the station, went down an escalator, and underground there was a small shopping area. People were queuing to buy tickets, just as Iván had warned me, though I already had mine. I wasn’t sure which platform the bus departed from, so I asked a French woman who told me platforms 5 to 7.

Since there were still 30 minutes left, I wandered into a very peculiar shop with Buddha statues, incense, journals, and souvenirs. When I entered, the owner asked me to leave my backpack at the entrance so I wouldn’t knock things over.

We spoke for a long time. He was a hippie. I asked if he was an artisan because he was making a bracelet. I told him I was coming from a festival about looking into each other’s eyes and giving hugs, and he also shared things about his life. When we said goodbye, we hugged, and since he was wearing a mala around his neck, he told me I was leaving with the blessing of the Dalai Lama. (ToT)

Very meaningful, don’t you think?

He was a 78-year-old man with a gray beard and round glasses. He had suffered a stroke but had recovered; the only thing left was that he constantly felt like crying, always emotionally open. “Second Apapacho moment.” I told him that was exactly what I needed, because it aligned perfectly with my spiritual practice.

The bus took us along a winding road with horror-movie curves, especially when trucks came toward us invading our lane and one of the vehicles had to stop. Do you think it was the first time I could read on a bus, on a road like that, without getting motion sickness? Maybe it’s true that my energies are different now. At least, I feel it.

Once in the French village, I went looking for a hostel. I asked a couple for directions. It was closed, but the intercom told me I could leave my backpack inside and return at 4 p.m. for check-in.

I continued up the street and a guy from the bus queue approached me. He was desperate for a beer and wouldn’t stop talking. I entered the church and he came in too. I walked around a bit and then told him I was going to sit quietly for a while, so he left. I’m sure I’ll run into him again tomorrow.

I was able to practice my French (super happy about that). Even with poor pronunciation and lack of vocabulary, I managed pretty well. :)

At the top of the street I found the municipal refuge and was lucky enough to get a free bed for 15 euros. They asked me for my pilgrim credential and I had to collect it from the office. Luckily the man there spoke both Spanish and French; he gave me loads of information and a complete list of hostels for all the Camino stages.

I went into a shop to buy a hat and, as I turned a corner, I was struck by the intense aromas coming from a spice shop. I entered and it was a journey for the sense of smell. After spending quite a while there, I ended up buying a mix of nuts, raisins, and chia seeds called “Mélange énergétique.”

Later I passed by the river where many people were swimming. From there I wanted to go to Carrefour to buy some dinner. I ate, and now I’m thinking of showering and going to bed because tomorrow I have to wake up very early.

“Bonsoir,” or rather, “bonne nuit.”
